    "summary": "Jam‑heavy Dark Star > Alligator > Drums > The Eleven; tight New Speedway Boogie",
    "blurb": "The second set delivers a monster Dark Star > Alligator > Drums > The Eleven sequence and a fiery New Speedway Boogie. Jerry’s guitar is on fire while the rhythm section drives deep improvisations. Standout moments include Black Peter, Mason's Children and Uncle John's Band.",
    "review": "December 30, 1969 at Boston’s Tea Party captured one of the Dead’s most jam‑laden late‑’60s outings. The first set rolls through solid roots numbers – Good Lovin’, Mama Tried and a crisp New Speedway Boogie – before launching into an exploratory second half that truly shines. A pristine A+ soundboard (Charlie Miller’s mastering) lets you hear Jerry’s Gibson SG “fired up” with perfect tone, especially on the soaring Uncle John’s Band lead work, while the rhythm section locks in during the extended Dark Star > Alligator > Drums > The Eleven saga. Pigpen’s organ adds that laid‑back early‑’69 texture, and Bob Weir’s vocal punch on Me And My Uncle stands out. Highlights ripple through Mason’s Children, Black Peter and a tight, high‑energy New Speedway Boogie, making this show a must‑listen for fans of the band’s deep‑groove period.\n\nFor listeners seeking the definitive experience, the Miller soundboard (gd69-12-30.sbd.miller.28469.sbeok.shnf) offers crystal‑clear guitar and drum separation with only a minor vocal distortion hiccup on Uncle John’s Band. The recording captures every nuance of the band’s improvisational flow without the gaps or dropouts that plague other sources, cementing this night as one of the most compelling snapshots of the Dead’s evolving live chemistry.",
